{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1588 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "c0d9931202c3431e263281a4642dfb4f8adb81554f576386b507b7eee51cf8bb" :edb/db-before-t 1587 :edb/db-after-t 1588 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534900 50 #inst "2026-10-08T10:27:05.431Z" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171823 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n2. $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n3. $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186171823 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^2?$\n\n- $\\mathbf A(x,y) = \\sqrt{x}\\,\\mathbf i + y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf B(x,y,z) = \\ln y\\,\\mathbf i - (x+y)\\,\\mathbf j  + z\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf C(t) = \\sin{t}\\,\\mathbf i + (2-t^3)\\,\\mathbf j$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171823 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It maps every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\: \\mid \\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2$.\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$.\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534900 false] [17592186171823 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^2.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y)\\in \\mathbb R^2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf A(x,y)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2 \\:|\\: x \\geq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^2.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$ It is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2.$  It assigns points in $\\mathbb{R}$ (not $\\mathbb{R^2}$) to vectors in $\\mathbb{R^2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171824 1100 "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,$." 13194139534900 false] [17592186171824 1100 "Find the domain of the vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}\\, \\mathbf{i} + \\sqrt{1+x-y} \\, \\mathbf{j}\\,.\n$$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171824 1145 "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y}\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F$:\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n$x^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0$\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n$D_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}$.\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/2d11f66345e024eed7bf280882ed66a0.png)\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n$1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x$\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n$D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}$.\n\nThe region $D_2$, shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/4792228f471aa7edfa5498ff57e5d63b.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by\n\n$$\nD = D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: \\mid \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3343/Source/Images/fd17dd94e4234f271b92358cae0a9b6d.png)" 13194139534900 false] [17592186171824 1145 "The function $\\mathbf F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y) =\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2}, \\qquad Q(x,y) = \\sqrt{1+x-y} .\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $\\mathbf F\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\nx^2 + y^2 = 0 \\quad\\Longrightarrow \\quad x=y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_1 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq (0,0)\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_1$ is the entire plane $\\mathbb R^2$ with the origin removed, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/d4a418bc0dbfc61c0e55c494db51ee0fa8125b6b7056ccbe4e32ddca82574dc2.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive or zero. To find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n1+x - y \\geq 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y\\leq 1 + x\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain of $Q$ is given by\n\n$$\nD_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D_2,$ shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/242903cc94103f8bb9cdc190a229fe7e86395f44600e8318d728fea3bc339385.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $\\mathbf F$ is given by \n\n$$\nD =  D_1\\cap D_2 = \\big\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\: | \\: (x,y)\\neq 0,\\, y \\leq 1 + x\\big\\}.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/f02ca21443a81cdb039a3d97bab0b0f1036219bb89ecbdaa32386cbc4e5da6e3.png)" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171825 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n2. $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n3. $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186171825 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\n- $\\mathbf R(x,y,z) = 2\\,\\mathbf i - x\\,\\mathbf j + y\\,\\mathbf k$\n\n- $\\mathbf S(x,y) = 2x\\,\\mathbf i - y^3\\,\\mathbf j$\n\n- $\\mathbf T(x,y,z) = \\dfrac{x}{z}\\,\\mathbf i - \\dfrac{y}{z}\\,\\mathbf j + \\dfrac{z}{2}\\,\\mathbf k$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186171825 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3$. A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}$.\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$. It assigns every point in the set $S$, given by\n$S = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\: \\mid \\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}$\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534900 false] [17592186171825 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $\\mathbf F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $\\mathbf F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $\\mathbf R(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z) \\in \\mathbb{R^3}$ to a vector in $\\mathbb{R^3}.$\n\n- The function $\\mathbf S(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$) to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $\\mathbf T(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3\\:|\\: z \\neq 0\\big\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in $\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172017 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318354 13194139534900 true] [17592186172017 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318361 13194139534900 true] [17592186172017 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318396 13194139534900 true] [17592186172018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318368 13194139534900 true] [17592186172018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318375 13194139534900 true] [17592186172018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318382 13194139534900 true] [17592186172018 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318389 13194139534900 true] [17592186172019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318403 13194139534900 true] [17592186172019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318410 13194139534900 true] [17592186172019 1020 #edb/ref 17592186318417 13194139534900 true] [17592186172210 1017 #edb/ref 17592186168463 13194139534900 false] [17592186172210 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318342 13194139534900 true] [17592186172210 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534900 true] [17592186172210 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = \\ln   y \\, \\mathbf{i} - {e}^{x y} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186172210 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $G(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $H(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{xy}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172210 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ to a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172211 1017 #edb/ref 17592186168469 13194139534900 false] [17592186172211 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318348 13194139534900 true] [17592186172211 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534900 true] [17592186172211 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{G} ( t ) = {e}^{t} \\, \\mathbf{i} - t \\, \\mathbf{j} + \\sin   t \\, \\mathbf{k}$\n2. $\\mathbf{H} ( x , y ) = {x}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{i} - {y}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n3. $\\mathbf{K} ( x , y , z ) = \\cot   x \\, \\mathbf{i} - y \\, \\mathbf{j} + z \\, \\mathbf{k}$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186172211 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $G(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$\n- $H(x,y)={x}^{2}\\,i-{y}^{2}\\,j$\n- $K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172211 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $G(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $H(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172213 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534900 true] [17592186172213 1100 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on $R^{2}$, given by\n\n$\\mathbf{F} ( x , y ) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9 - {x}^{2} - {y}^{2}}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + \\frac{x y}{{x}^{2} + {y}^{2}} \\, \\mathbf{j} \\, .$." 13194139534900 false] [17592186172213 1100 "Determine which of the following shaded regions depicts the domain of the vector field $F$ on ${R}^{2},$ given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\\,.\n$$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172213 1145 "The function $F$ can be expressed in terms of its components $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j,\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}},\\,Q(x,y)=\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe first find the domain of each component function. Then, we compute their intersection to find the domain of $F:$\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is defined when the expression under the radical is positive. So, to find the domain, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}>0\\,⟹\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<9.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{1},$ which is the interior of a circle of radius $3,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/e54618f7e72d70f299686ca2846a1d6fa0caccb8daecb090b3a653271c88fcd3.png)\n\n- The function $Q(x,y)$ is defined everywhere other than when the denominator vanishes. To find where the denominator vanishes, we set it equal to zero and solve:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=y=0.\n$$\n\nThis defines the region ${D}_{2},$ which is the entire plane ${R}^{2}$ with the origin removed:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/5b77ad4e9574e94e6a05eee7680f4b57d0190d40583c37c2097acef958cab7e2.png)\n\nFinally, the domain $D$ of $F$ is given by ${D}_{1}\\cap {D}_{2},$ shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14042874/assets/30462ed1f25749ac4ef05cf9ca9573508ae28a0799d7986dd005f71fdac15679.png)" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172215 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534900 true] [17592186172215 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{2}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{P} ( x , y ) = \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\, \\mathbf{i} + ( x + y {)}^{2} \\, \\mathbf{j}$\n2. $\\mathbf{Q} ( x , y , z ) = y \\, \\mathbf{i} - x y \\, \\mathbf{j} + ( z + 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{k}$\n3. $\\mathbf{R} ( t ) = ( t + 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{i} - ( 3 t - 1 ) \\, \\mathbf{j}$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186172215 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,i+(x+y)^{2}\\,j$\n- $Q(x,y,z)=y\\,i-xy\\,j+(z+1)\\,k$\n- $R(t)=(t+1)\\,i-(3t-1)\\,j$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172215 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $P(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x\\ne 0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $Q(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $R(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It assigns points in  $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186168505 13194139534900 false] [17592186172217 1017 #edb/ref 17592186318424 13194139534900 true] [17592186172217 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534900 true] [17592186172217 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on $R^{3}$?\n\n1. $\\mathbf{U} ( x , y , z ) = \\langle  x + 2 , \\sqrt{x - y} , \\ln   z \\rangle$\n2. $\\mathbf{V} ( x , y ) = \\langle  x {e}^{y} , 1 - y \\rangle$\n3. $\\mathbf{W} ( t ) = \\langle  {t}^{2} , 2 t , 2 - 3 t \\rangle$" 13194139534900 false] [17592186172217 1100 "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\n- $U(x,y,z)=⟨x+2,\\sqrt{x-y},\\operatorname{ln}⁡z⟩$\n- $V(x,y)=⟨x{e}^{y},1-y⟩$\n- $W(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t,2-3t⟩$" 13194139534900 true] [17592186172217 1145 "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $U(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ge y,\\,z>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $V(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $W(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318343 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318344 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318345 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318346 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318347 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1041 "selection" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1104 #edb/ref 17592186318346 13194139534900 true] [17592186318342 1110 #uuid "1f5ae6d6-1977-505a-8208-c6ef97d6f3e3" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318343 1034 "I, II, and III" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318343 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534900 true] [17592186318343 1111 #uuid "e085e56c-ab04-5255-8f2e-e486bf529732" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318344 1034 "I only" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318344 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534900 true] [17592186318344 1111 #uuid "8f8a7227-1927-51df-b1cd-eaa236149836" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318345 1034 "III only" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318345 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534900 true] [17592186318345 1111 #uuid "01f45662-f409-5414-a3a4-14a855652d5f" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318346 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318346 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534900 true] [17592186318346 1111 #uuid "66bb3258-d9ab-5a7a-a63f-c101903168af" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318347 1034 "II only" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318347 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534900 true] [17592186318347 1111 #uuid "ffe0d6ca-418d-5609-8c74-0abb8404b00d" 13194139534900 true] [17592186318348 1000 #edb/ref 17592186318349 13194139534900 true] [17592186318348 1000 #edb/ref 1